**Quicksort and Mergesort: Worst-Case Complexity Comparison**
===========================================================

Two popular sorting algorithms are Quicksort and Mergesort. While both have an average-case complexity of O(n log n), their worst-case complexity differs significantly.

**Quicksort Worst-Case Complexity**
---------------------------------

Quicksort's worst-case complexity is O(n^2), which occurs when the pivot is always the smallest or largest element in the array. This can happen when the input array is already sorted or nearly sorted in a particular order.

For example, if the input array is `1, 2, 3, 4, 5`, and we always choose the first element as the pivot, the algorithm will recurse with smaller and smaller subarrays, leading to a quadratic number of comparisons.

**Mergesort Worst-Case Complexity**
----------------------------------

Mergesort's worst-case complexity is O(n log n), even when the input array is already sorted or nearly sorted. This is because Mergesort always divides the array into two halves and merges them, resulting in a logarithmic number of recursive calls.

**Why Mergesort Avoids Worst-Case Complexity**
--------------------------------------------

Mergesort's stability and the fact that it always divides the array into two halves contribute to its O(n log n) worst-case complexity. When merging two sorted subarrays, Mergsort only needs to compare adjacent elements, resulting in a logarithmic number of comparisons.

In contrast, Quicksort's worst-case performance is highly dependent on the choice of the pivot, which can lead to a quadratic number of comparisons.

**Example Code (Java)**
```java
// Quicksort worst-case complexity example
public class QuickSortWorstCase {
    public static void quickSort(int[] arr) {
        quickSortRecursive(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    private static void quickSortRecursive(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low >= high) return;
        int pivot = arr[low];
        int i = low + 1;
        int j = high;
        while (i <= j) {
            while (i <= j && arr[i] <= pivot) i++;
            while (i <= j && arr[j] > pivot) j--;
            if (i <= j) {
                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
                i++;
                j--;
            }
        }
        int temp2 = arr[low];
        arr[low] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp2;
        quickSortRecursive(arr, low, j - 1);
        quickSortRecursive(arr, j + 1, high);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        quickSort(arr);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
    }
}

// Mergesort implementation (for reference)
public class MergeSort {
    public static void mergeSort(int[] arr) {
        mergeSortRecursive(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    private static void mergeSortRecursive(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low >= high) return;
        int mid = (low + high) / 2;
        mergeSortRecursive(arr, low, mid);
        mergeSortRecursive(arr, mid + 1, high);
        merge(arr, low, mid, high);
    }

    private static void merge(int[] arr, int low, int mid, int high) {
        int[] left = Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, low, mid + 1);
        int[] right = Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, mid + 1, high + 1);
        int i = 0, j = 0, k = low;
        while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
            if (left[i] <= right[j]) {
                arr[k] = left[i];
                i++;
            } else {
                arr[k] = right[j];
                j++;
            }
            k++;
        }
        while (i < left.length) {
            arr[k] = left[i];
            i++;
            k++;
        }
        while (j < right.length) {
            arr[k] = right[j];
            j++;
            k++;
        }
    }
}
```
